{"parentUuid":"b4c8df71-cc4f-4b0b-8c58-3b0e6a404543","isSidechain":false,"type":"user","promptId":"5361ae39-a4fe-4d3c-b1dd-820944964160","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_s06_001","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I reproduced it: with `tidepool follow /tmp/app.log` running, `logrotate -f` with the default create mode stops all output.\n\nRoot cause: the follower puts an fsnotify watch on the file itself. logrotate renames app.log to app.log.1 and creates a new app.log, so the watch keeps following the old inode and never sees the new file. For any follow-style feature in tidepool the rule is: watch the directory and reopen on CREATE events for the followed name (on RENAME, drain the old handle first), which is what `tail -F` does.\n\nI changed internal/watch/follow.go accordingly and added TestFollowSurvivesRotate, which renames and recreates the file; it passes with the rest of ./internal/watch. I did not touch the copytruncate path."}]}]},"toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Users report that `tidepool follow <file>` stops printing new lines after logrotate rotates the file. Reproduce it in /home/user/tidepool, find the root cause, fix it with a test, and report what you changed.","agentId":"df36a96ff92272ab","totalDurationMs":204358,"totalToolUseCount":23},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b4c8df71-cc4f-4b0b-8c58-3b0e6a404543","timestamp":"2026-09-19T14:23:55.809Z","uuid":"757e656d-b0ed-4f2e-bb6e-0824e18ecba7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/user/tidepool","sessionId":"a068f531-c0cc-41d5-9da7-478ce69a6bbe","version":"2.1.300","gitBranch":"main"}
